  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B058",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B013",
    "B005"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks unite a beautiful quantum idea with an experimentally exacting realization: atoms can be confined while the magic wavelength suppresses the trapping perturbation that would otherwise compromise the clock transition. Katori's concept and Ye's pioneering realization provide a focused attribution. The achievement is no longer a prospective instrument claim: independent laboratories have reproduced and extended it, establishing a new frontier in time and frequency metrology and a sensitive means of probing fundamental physics.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation is whether the achievement should be regarded as a sufficiently discrete fundamental advance rather than an outstanding continuing development in precision instrumentation, and whether two names can adequately represent a large clock community.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson judges optical lattice clocks a major advance but notes that their ultimate foundational and practical consequences are still developing.",
        "response": "That qualification is fair, especially concerning eventual time-standard deployment. But a prize need not await every application: the core physical architecture, its reproducible performance, and its use for fundamental tests are already mature. The relevant achievement is the lattice-clock principle and realization, not promises about its eventual applications."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson emphasizes that Katori originated the magic-wavelength concept and Ye made pioneering experimental advances, while cautioning that credit extends beyond the two scientists.",
        "response": "I agree with both parts. The historical record must distinguish the core concept and early decisive realizations from subsequent performance improvements, but that is precisely why this two-person attribution is more defensible than crediting the whole later metrology enterprise."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson makes a strong case for aberration-corrected electron optics as a long-mature, widely deployed advance whose historical attribution also requires care.",
        "response": "It is an excellent alternative, but I give priority to lattice clocks because their controlled atomic transitions make the measurement advance itself a direct new probe of quantum physics, rather than principally an extension of an established imaging technique."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The archival boundary between Katori and Ye's foundational contributions and those of other early lattice-clock pioneers should be reviewed.",
      "The citation should avoid claiming credit for later performance records or an eventual redefinition of the second."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
